AUTO_MEMORIES: STRUCTURED_MEMORIES, register files replaced by a generated macro, by name

bazel-orfs carries this against ORFS; it is not upstreamed.

A many-port register file synthesised to flops is what a legaliser
cannot spread, and a core in XiangShan's class has these as compiled
bitcells. bazel-orfs's tools/structured_gen builds one as a placed
standard-cell macro from a small spec. This is the hook that lets a
design say which modules get that treatment.

STRUCTURED_MEMORIES lists spec files, one per module. Nothing is
detected: XiangShan's register files are Reg(Vec) and never become a
yosys memory, and a Mem rewrite would change which writer wins.
gen_memories.py runs the generator (STRUCTURED_GEN, set by the build
system) per spec with --check-ports against the Verilog file declaring
the module, writes <module>.lef/.lib/_pre_layout.lib beside the
AUTO_MEMORIES views, appends the module to blackboxes.txt after FakeRAM
has written it, and records what it did in structured.json. A spec
whose ports are not the module's stops the build there.

Retires when ORFS grows a way to declare a generated macro per module;
then `//:bump` onto that commit drops this patch.

